{%- comment -%}
  PG Cards: framing fixes for individual product-card images on the homepage.

  The Crystal Legends Orb hero is 1792x1800 -- square, in a square card -- so
  nothing is being cropped. The problem is the composition: the orb cluster
  occupies the top two thirds and the bottom third is its reflection, so at card
  size the scene reads small and half the tile is a mirror. Scaling from a high
  origin fills the card with the orbs and crops the reflection away.

  Keyed on the image filename rather than a class, because the homepage builds
  its own cards inside pg-home and its class names are its own. Scoped to #pgh
  so the product page gallery, the cart thumbnails and the recommendation rail
  all keep the full frame -- only the homepage tile is re-framed.

  The parent gets overflow:hidden via :has(), whatever element it turns out to
  be, so the scaled image cannot spill outside the tile.

  transform-origin is the framing control, and it is worth being precise about
  which way it moves things. The origin is the point that stays put while the
  image grows around it, so the visible window ends up centred on it: a LOWER
  percentage keeps the top of the image and pushes the rest down out of the
  tile, which is what "pull the image down" means on screen. At 15% the window
  sits over the orb cluster and the reflection band falls away below.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
  #pgh *:has(> img[src*="hf_20260812_024500"]){overflow:hidden !important}
  #pgh img[src*="hf_20260812_024500"]{
    transform:scale(1.3) !important;
    transform-origin:50% 15% !important;
  }
</style>
